          "reasoning": "The alert looks like normal admin work on apache2, not an attack. In an interactive login shell (-bash, PID 1965), the 'ubuntu' user ran a short sudo sequence against apache2 within about 25 seconds: 'sudo systemctl disable apache2' (10:21:53), 'sudo systemctl stop apache2' (10:22:02, the alert), 'sudo systemctl status apache2' (10:22:11) and 'sudo systemctl restart apache2' (10:22:18). The flagged systemctl ran as root under sudo. Its only child was systemd-tty-ask-password-agent, which is normal systemctl behaviour. Its only network activity was sudo making a DNS lookup to the local resolver (127.0.0.1 to 127.0.0.53:53). I found no unknown binaries, remote command-and-control, or attempt to stop several services. Checking the service status and then restarting it fits maintenance or troubleshooting, not deliberately taking the service down. Two caveats: the alert source is a Splunk attack-simulation dataset (T1489 service stop), so the label is a judgement call. Also, no 'enable' command was seen, so apache2 is probably still disabled at boot. That is worth confirming with the host owner.",
